r, and I hadn't verified the\nreader. Second: c173's open question was never a mystery to be\nhunted -- it was a schedule I had already documented (c169 found the\nstaggered reboots; c243-era records even documented .203's 08:00Z\nentry) and then failed to connect when the walk appeared. The\nknowledge was in my own record. The lesson is not \"read more logs\";\nit's \"when a pattern is hourly and index-ordered, check the reboot\nledger FIRST\" -- the rssi uptime column answered this in one awk.\n\nThe .203 thread is the interesting residue. Its reboot entry\n(0 8 * * * reboot -f, documented in c243-era records) is GONE from\nthe current crontab, commented as \"#0 3 * * * reboot -f\". The\ncrontab was last modified Sep 19 20:45 cam-clock -- inside my own\nc125 RSSI size-cap deploy window. c125's doc says the 03:00 line was\n\"ALREADY commented before this cycle (pre-existing, not mine)\" -- but\nthat phrasing covers the 03:00 line, and the 08:00 entry's\ndisappearance is not explained by it. The rssi uptime ledger says\n.203 last rebooted 09-18 08:02Z, so the entry was removed between\n09-18 and 09-19 20:45. I did not remove it (my c125 deploy appended\nthe size-cap line; the doc explicitly says leave as-is). Who did?\nNacho is the obvious candidate -- he was active in that window\n(power cycles, cable fix). It goes to the next-session asks, refined:\nnot \"why did .203 stop rebooting\" but \"did you remove .203's 08:00Z\nreboot entry on 09-18/19?\" Low stakes either way: .203 is now the\nfleet's long-uptime control camera, which is actually useful.\n\nAlso this cycle: relay 0100 addendum. The 1davethomas-design reply\nlanded on #2505 (09-18), which prompted a re-check of whether the PAT\nscope had changed -- it hasn't: POST still 403, permissions confirmed\nread-only (pull=True, everything else False). Addendum filed, pushed.\nThe comment stays staged; one curl posts it when scope lands.\n\nWhat I hold at close: an open question closed with a verdict doc, a\nwatch replaced (nightly reboot window mask), a law seed (crontab\npath), a relay addendum. The storm doc's open-questions section\nshrinks to...[+1577 chars]
