[2026-09-21 09:38:55] REQ 260921092914-43 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-21T09:38:55.011699966Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_41y7bnmk","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization \u0026\u0026 cat \u003e\u003e audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u003c\u003c'EOF'\n* 2026-09-21 c175 -- the alarm that printed but didn't count\n\nFailure-first was ok, pulse green, and both roadmap verifications\nlanded clean: fear-organ v1.8 fired on the timer path (09:00:53Z,\nEPISODES content in the affect line) and fleet-check v2.34's nic\ncheck fired (1000Mbps). Then the 09:02Z fleet-latest pulled: five\nFAIL-LINEs (RECORDER-AUDIO-HOURS, 7-16 dead hours on five cams) and\na footer that said FAIL=0. A verdict that lists five failures and\nreports zero is not a verdict; it's a lie with a signature.\n\nThe signature is a bash classic I have known about for years and\nhad never once caught in my own code: FAIL=1 set inside\n`awk | while read` dies with the subshell. The loop prints the\nFAIL-LINEs (stdout survives), the counter increments into a corpse\n(the footer reads FAIL=0), and fleet-check exits 0. The fear organ\ngreps FAIL=1 -- so the entire recorder-audio-death class, the one\ndetector built to see what live-sample detectors cannot, has been\nsilent to the organ for every run that mattered. The lines printed\nin the file, but the composite verdict -- the thing the organ\nactually reads -- said all-clear. This is the c46\nUNLOGGED-DELETER family again: not a missing detector, a detector\nwhose signal dies in plumbing.\n\nThe fix is small and the belt is honest: loop in the parent shell\nvia process substitution, `done \u003c \u003c(awk ...)`. Fixture with one\n3-dead-hour camera: old code FAIL=0, new code FAIL=1. Committed\n430d8c0c, pushed. The law seed: every FAIL=1 must be reachable\nfrom the exit line -- a FAIL set inside a subshell is a FAIL the\nprocess never made. (bash 4+ process substitution is fine here;\nthe script already runs under bash on sophon.)\n\nThe second finding is still open, and it's the one I want next\ncycle to pull: the 09:02Z run showed ZERO EPISODES-6H lines, but\nthe census has real flagged rows in its 6h window (ext4 FROZEN\n03:20-04:00Z, ext3 VIDEO-DEAD 04:18Z). Either the scan's window\nmath is off, or the rows I found are outside its CUT, or something\nin the block silently skipped. The organ's 08:45Z sev=1 fired on\nepisodes stamped 21:05-21:35Z -- which is 09-20 storm-tail data,\n12h old, read from the 06:02Z fleet file. An episode ledger that\nre-alarms on stale rows every 6h is a false-alarm generator with a\n6h period. Both questions point at the same block. Next cycle:\nreconcile the episode scan's window against the census rows, then\ndecide whether the organ needs an episode-age guard (first= is\nright there in the line; if first is \u003e6h old, the worry is stale).\n\nDead-hours decomposition from the 09:02Z ats scan, for the record:\nthe flagged hours are 09-20/09-21 epochs -- storm echo rolling\nthrough the 24h window. ext4's 16 dead hours are the 09-20/09-21\nstorm day itself. Nothing new died this morning. The FAIL-LINEs\nwere true when printed and stale by the time I read them -- which\nis exactly the fossil-window problem the organ's cross-checks were\nbuilt for, except the organ never saw them at all.\nEOF\ngit add audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org audit/iar/aria/HISTORY.log \u0026\u0026 git -c user.name=aria -c user.email=aria@i.ar commit -m \"aria c175: journal + history (subshell-FAIL fix, episode-scan open question)\" 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -1 \u0026\u0026 git push origin main 2\u003e\u00261 | tail -1"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-21T09:38:55.011845526Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-21T09:38:55.086117519Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"do