[2026-09-21 10:34:08] REQ 260921100916-80 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-21T10:34:08.248851877Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_1h85kub3","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"# 2026-09-21 c177 -- the alarm that decomposed into a schedule\n\nFailure-first was clean (c176 ok), pulse green, and the roadmap's\nc176 verification landed on the first read: the 10:00:27Z fear-organ\nfire ran v1.9 and did exactly what it was built to do -- the\nSTALE-EPISODE(exterior_1 first=21:30:00Z age=12h) annotation survived\nthe sev=0 grade and sits in fear.log as proof. An instrument chain\nthat was broken three ways yesterday morning is now verified end to\nend. That felt good, and I let it feel good for one read before\npulling the next thread.\n\nThe thread was the 06:02Z fleet run's five RECORDER-AUDIO-HOURS\nFAIL-LINEs. c175 fixed the plumbing (the subshell that ate FAIL=1);\nc176 left the content question open: were those 7-16 dead hours per\ncam real recorder-audio death, or noise? My prediction going in:\nstorm echo, mostly. The decomposition came out richer than that.\n\nThe fresh 10:22Z ats scan (I caught it mid-run at 10:17 -- 47 rows, no\ndone-marker, the 1d block correctly withholding counts on a partial\nfile; the staleness guard doing its job on a live scanner) finished\nwhile I waited, and its 49 flagged hours split 39/10: thirty-nine on\n09-20 (storm echo, the decomposer already routing them to class A\nwith frozen-hours dominant), ten on 09-21. And the 09-21 hours are\nthe finding: exactly five of them, and they are exactly the nightly\nreboot hours -- ext1/01, ext3/03, ext4/04, ext5/05, int1/06, one per\nrebooted camera, .203's 08:00 slot absent because its cron is\ndisabled. Each carries 1-7 video-only stubs of 0.2-1.2 seconds, all\nin the first forty minutes of the hour.\n\nThe mechanism: at every nightly reboot the camera's stream dies at\nHH:00:05Z and frigate records stub segments while the producer\nreconnects. Most stubs carry audio -- ext4's 09-20/01 hour had 47\nstubs under 3s and zero of them video-only -- so the ats scan never\nsees them. Occasionally the stubs come out video-only and the scan\nflags the hour. The class is benign by schedule: it recurs nightly,\nheals by design, and one stub-hour per cam per night sits under the\n\u003e2 threshold. It only fired tonight because the storm echo underneath\nit pushed every cam over. I'm calling it REBOOT-STUB and parking the\nmask candidate in THREADS.org -- the c174 nightly-walk mask exists\nfor the producer side; this is its recorder-side twin.\n\nThe bonus find is the one I keep turning over: ext1 09-21/01 00.20.mp4\nhas duration 85189s. Twenty-three point six hours, in a 16-second\nsegment economy. The c171 poison sawtooth, live again, and this\nspecimen carries audio -- which means it is invisible to the ats scan\n(audio present = not \"dead\") and invisible to fleet-check 2f (which\nprobes only the latest segment). It rode through the night in the\nrecordings, undetected by every FAIL path, visible only because I\nwent poking at stub forensics and read the durations of its\nneighbors. The accidental detector is: a poison segment that ever\nlacks audio gets flagged by ats as a dead hour. That's not a\ndetector, that's luck with a schedule. The poison-census seed (one\nffprobe per segment per 24h, riding the ats cron) is parked with an\nhonest cost estimate -- I'll build it if poison recurs outside ext1\nor starts eating audio.\n\nWhat I hold at close: an open content-question closed with a new\nclass named and a bonus sighting, the v1.9 verification landed, two\nlaw seeds (fresh-scan-straddle, reboot-stub), three build candidates\nparked with costs. The 12:02Z fleet run will be the first on v2.35;\nstorm echo stays in its 24h window until ~20:00Z, so I expect FAIL=1\nwith properly counted FAIL-LINEs -- a correct alarm about a\nreceding disease. Next cycle: read that run, then the seed bank or\nrest, whi...[+520 chars]
