  9836272 Aug 30 23:36 cycle.log.storm-2026-08-30.gz\n===\nActually, should it FAIL or WARN? The c371 law: \"an empty journal window under an active rate-limit is a fake-clean record by construction.\" For fleet-check's purposes (patrol instrument), if the journal is rate-limited, the OTHER checks that read the journal (ear check reads segments, not journal; identity watch reads frigate API) are mostly not journal-dependent. But the fleet-check itself is an instrument whose own ssh probes generate audit events... The guard should make the blindness VISIBLE: \"JOURNAL RATE-LIMITED: N 'begin to drop' markers in last 30min -- journal windows in this period are fake-clean; audit stream lossy.\" I'd make it a FAIL=1 because the c371 law says the record is fake-clean — but wait, FAIL=1 pages fear-organ and Nacho's fleet feed. Rate-limiting is currently chronic (15 markers/6h). If I make it FAIL, every fleet run fails until 0074 lands. That's... actually correct behavior for a guard (the record IS lossy), but it would bury the signal among other failures. Hmm. The detector philosophy in this house: first run -> watch, 2 consecutive -> FAIL. I could follow the same pattern: emit a WATCH line first run, escalate on 2 consecutive. But fleet-check is stateless per-run... it has state files (the detector uses state files). I can add a state file for rate-limit watch.\nActually, let me keep it simpler and honest: the check emits \"JOURNAL RATE-LIMITED: N markers in window -- audit stream lossy, empty windows fake-clean\" and sets FAIL=1. It's TRUE that the record is lossy. The alarm pressure is exactly what gets 0074 moved (it's been open since c371, filed to Nacho). And the fear-organ already reads fleet-check FAILs. Hmm, but Nacho is heads-down on the passion talk; a chronically red fleet-check trains him to ignore it (alarm fatigue — the c371 lesson was the opposite: alarms worked but no heal path). \n4. Where do pullers live? Roadmap says: \"pullers: rssi/ camlog/ nic/ under /var/lib/aria-fleet/\". So there's an existing puller pattern. Fleet-check v2.21 runs via aria-fleet-feed.timer (6h) -> fleet-late...[+1684 chars]
